  'RIG-TUNE': Fact('RIG-TUNE', 'Placing a few expert layers on the GPUs and adding a multi-token-prediction draft raised generation from 14 to 20.5 tokens/s; raising the RAM speed from 4,400 to 5,200 MT/s then gave 21.7 tokens/s (measured RAM bandwidth at 4,400 MT/s: about 62 GB/s)', sources: ['SRC-HOMELAB'], value: '14 → 20.5 → 21.7 tok/s'),
  'RIG-OC': Fact('RIG-OC', 'Overclocking the GPUs barely helped (21.8 → 22.0 tokens/s), and an undervolt kept the same speed with about 15 W less per GPU: with experts in RAM, system memory is the bottleneck', sources: ['SRC-HOMELAB'], value: '+1% / −15 W'),
  'RIG-SMALL': Fact('RIG-SMALL', 'The small server\'s 16 GB GPU runs a 27B-class model at about 55 tokens/s with a 96K-token context', sources: ['SRC-HOMELAB'], value: '~55 tok/s'),
  'RIG-BURN': Fact('RIG-BURN', 'A GPU stress test with too much memory overclock hung the card; the stuck test kept it at about 397 W and 83 °C for about 10 hours before anyone noticed', sources: ['SRC-HOMELAB'], value: '397 W, 10 h'),
  'RIG-VRAMTEMP': Fact('RIG-VRAMTEMP', 'Under a stress test at stock settings, one RTX 3090\'s memory reached 104 °C within about 2.5 minutes (core only 75 °C); 12 of its 24 memory chips sit on the back of the card, cooled only by the backplate', sources: ['SRC-HOMELAB'], value: '104 °C'),
  'RIG-SECUREBOOT': Fact('RIG-SECUREBOOT', 'A power cut reset the BIOS and switched Secure Boot back on; the unsigned NVIDIA driver was then refused at boot and the GPUs disappeared until the setting was fixed and saved in a BIOS profile', sources: ['SRC-HOMELAB']),
  'RUN-SPEC-T5': Fact('RUN-SPEC-T5', 'Speculative decoding (Leviathan et al.): a small draft model guesses several tokens, the big model checks them all in one pass; on T5-XXL it ran 2X-3X faster "with identical outputs"', sources: ['SRC-SPECDEC'], value: '2-3×'),
  'RUN-SPEC-FORMULA': Fact('RUN-SPEC-FORMULA', 'With acceptance rate α and γ drafted tokens, each big-model pass yields on average (1 − α^(γ+1)) ÷ (1 − α) tokens; the wall-clock speed-up divides that by (γ·c + 1), where c is the draft\'s cost relative to the big model', sources: ['SRC-SPECDEC'], value: 'E = (1−α^(γ+1))/(1−α)'),
  'RUN-MEDUSA': Fact('RUN-MEDUSA', 'Medusa adds extra decoding heads to the model itself instead of a separate draft model: over 2.2× speed-up without changing output quality (Medusa-1), 2.3-2.8× when trained together with the model (Medusa-2)', sources: ['SRC-MEDUSA'], value: '2.2-2.8×'),
  'RUN-MTP-DSV3': Fact('RUN-MTP-DSV3', 'DeepSeek-V3\'s multi-token prediction head guesses the next-but-one token; that guess is accepted 85-90% of the time, giving about 1.8× the tokens per second', sources: ['SRC-DEEPSEEKV3'], value: '85-90% → 1.8×'),
  'RUN-QP-TABLE': Fact('RUN-QP-TABLE', 'Llama 3 8B, measured against the 16-bit original (llama.cpp scoreboard, RTX 4090): Q8_0 7.96 GiB, KLD 0.0014, same top token 97.7%; Q6_K 6.14 GiB, 0.0055, 96.0%; Q4_K_M 4.58 GiB, 0.031, 91.9%; Q2_K 2.96 GiB, 0.445, 71.1%', sources: ['SRC-LLAMACPP-PPL'], value: 'Q4_K_M: 91.9% same top token'),
  'RUN-QP-IMATRIX': Fact('RUN-QP-IMATRIX', 'An importance matrix helps at the same size: Llama 3 8B Q4_K_M KL divergence 0.0313 without vs 0.0282 with one; Q3_K_M 0.102 vs 0.084; Q2_K 0.445 vs 0.331', sources: ['SRC-LLAMACPP-PPL'], value: 'lower KLD with imatrix'),
  'RUN-QP-L2L3': Fact('RUN-QP-L2L3', 'The same quant hurts Llama 3 8B more than Llama 2 7B: at Q4_K_M the top token stays the same 94.7% of the time for Llama 2 but 91.9% for Llama 3 (KLD 0.0127 vs 0.0313)', sources: ['SRC-LLAMACPP-PPL'], value: '94.7% vs 91.9%'),
  'RT-LLAMACPP': Fact('RT-LLAMACPP', 'llama.cpp: "LLM inference in C/C++"; Apple silicon is "a first-class citizen" (Metal), CUDA for NVIDIA, HIP for AMD, plus Vulkan and SYCL; runs GGUF files; llama-server offers an OpenAI-compatible API', sources: ['SRC-LLAMACPP', 'SRC-LLAMACPP-SERVER'], value: 'GGUF, everywhere'),
  'RT-OLLAMA': Fact('RT-OLLAMA', 'Ollama: "Start building with open models"; runs on macOS, Windows and Linux; pulls models from its library or imports GGUF and Safetensors; REST API plus OpenAI compatibility', sources: ['SRC-OLLAMA'], value: 'easy local runner'),
  'RT-LMSTUDIO': Fact('RT-LMSTUDIO', 'LM Studio: desktop app "with MLX and llama.cpp under the hood"; downloads models from Hugging Face; can "Serve local models on OpenAI-like endpoints, locally and on the network"; supports MCP servers', sources: ['SRC-LMSTUDIO'], value: 'desktop app'),
  'RT-VLLM': Fact('RT-VLLM', 'vLLM: "Easy, fast, and cheap LLM serving for everyone"; PagedAttention; OpenAI-compatible API server; NVIDIA, AMD and Intel GPUs, CPUs and plugins for TPUs and other accelerators', sources: ['SRC-VLLM-README'], value: 'serving many users'),
  'RT-EXLLAMA': Fact('RT-EXLLAMA', 'ExLlamaV3: "an inference library for running local LLMs on modern consumer GPUs"; its own EXL3 quant format (based on QTIP), 2-8-bit cache quantization, tensor- and expert-parallel inference, CPU offloading for MoE', sources: ['SRC-EXLLAMAV3'], value: 'NVIDIA consumer GPUs'),
  'RT-MLX': Fact('RT-MLX', 'MLX LM: "a Python package for generating text and fine-tuning large language models on Apple silicon with MLX"; uses models from the Hugging Face Hub, can quantize them, LoRA and full fine-tuning', sources: ['SRC-MLXLM'], value: 'Apple silicon'),
  'CU-LORA-10K': Fact('CU-LORA-10K', 'LoRA on GPT-3 175B: compared with full fine-tuning with Adam, 10,000 times fewer trainable parameters and 3 times less GPU memory, with on-par or better quality', sources: ['SRC-LORA'], value: '10,000× / 3×'),
  'CU-LORA-MATH': Fact('CU-LORA-MATH', 'A LoRA update for a d × d weight matrix is the product of two thin matrices (d × r and r × d): 2·d·r trainable numbers instead of d², e.g. d = 4,096, r = 8 → 65,536 instead of 16.8 million (0.39%)', sources: ['SRC-LORA'], value: '2dr vs d²'),
  'CU-QLORA-48': Fact('CU-QLORA-48', 'QLoRA (4-bit frozen base + LoRA adapters) cut the memory to fine-tune a 65B model from more than 780 GB to under 48 GB, a single 48 GB GPU, while keeping 16-bit fine-tuning quality', sources: ['SRC-QLORA'], value: '>780 → <48 GB'),
  'CU-TASKVEC': Fact('CU-TASKVEC', 'A task vector = fine-tuned weights minus the original weights of the same model; task vectors can be added (combine skills) or negated (remove a behaviour)', sources: ['SRC-TASKARITH'], value: 'weights − base'),
  'CU-TIES': Fact('CU-TIES', 'TIES-merging resolves interference in three steps: trim each task vector to its largest changes, elect one sign per parameter, and merge only the values that agree with it', sources: ['SRC-TIES'], value: 'trim, elect, merge'),
  'CU-DARE': Fact('CU-DARE', 'DARE randomly drops a share p of the fine-tuning changes and rescales the rest by 1/(1 − p); fine-tuning changes are tiny (within 0.002) and 90% or even 99% can be dropped', sources: ['SRC-DARE'], value: '90-99% dropped'),
  'CU-REFUSAL-DIR': Fact('CU-REFUSAL-DIR', 'In 13 popular open chat models up to 72B, refusing is controlled by a single direction inside the model: erasing it stops refusals of harmful requests, adding it makes the model refuse even harmless ones; the authors say this shows how brittle current safety fine-tuning is', sources: ['SRC-REFUSALDIR'], value: '1 direction, 13 models'),
  'CU-CAA': Fact('CU-CAA', 'Contrastive Activation Addition: a steering vector is the average difference in the model\'s internal activations between examples with and without a behaviour; adding it (or subtracting it) at inference turns that behaviour up or down', sources: ['SRC-CAA'], value: 'add / subtract a vector'),
  'CU-LCPP-CV': Fact('CU-LCPP-CV', 'llama.cpp can apply control vectors at run time: --control-vector FNAME, --control-vector-scaled FNAME:SCALE, --control-vector-layer-range START END', sources: ['SRC-LLAMACPP-SERVER'], value: '--control-vector'),
  'SOTA-GPT6': Fact('SOTA-GPT6', 'OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (September 2026) is OpenAI\'s flagship; GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna followed on September 22 and GPT-6.1 Sol on September 29; available in ChatGPT and the OpenAI API', sources: ['SRC-OPENAI-GPT6'], value: 'Sep 2026, closed'),
  'SOTA-OPUS55': Fact('SOTA-OPUS55', 'Anthropic Claude Opus 5.5 (September 22, 2026), built for long-running agentic coding and knowledge work; released after testing by external evaluators', sources: ['SRC-ANTHROPIC-OPUS55'], value: 'Sep 22 2026, closed'),
  'SOTA-GEMINI4': Fact('SOTA-GEMINI4', 'Google Gemini 4 Argon, announced September 30, 2026 and generally available October 13; a 1 million token context', sources: ['SRC-GOOGLE-GEMINI4'], value: '1M tokens, closed'),
  'SOTA-MUSE': Fact('SOTA-MUSE', 'Meta Muse Wonder and Muse Wonder Full (October 12, 2026), which Meta calls "frontier models for agents"; closed, offered through the Meta Model API', sources: ['SRC-META-MUSE'], value: 'Oct 12 2026, closed'),
  'SOTA-DSV41': Fact('SOTA-DSV41', 'DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (September 2026, updated October 17): open weights under the MIT licence; mixture of experts with 552B backbone parameters (plus 196B Engram memory parameters), 8B active per token during prefill and 16B during decode; up to 1 million tokens; reads images and text', sources: ['SRC-HF-DSV41'], value: '552B, MIT'),
  'SOTA-QWEN38': Fact('SOTA-QWEN38', 'Qwen3.8 (August 2026): open-weight Qwen3.8-2.4T-A95B (2.4T total, 95B activated, 512 experts), Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (125B with 6B activated) and the dense Qwen3.8-27B (Apache 2.0); 262,144 tokens natively, extensible to 1,000,000', sources: ['SRC-HF-QWEN38'], value: '2.4T / 125B / 27B'),
